-- Fixture for the two EPISTEMIC knobs in the ladder visualizer:
--
--   * grounding        — how to read an atom nobody answered:
--                        'show what's known' (Kleene) / closed world / open world
--   * respect defaults — whether the TYPICALLY presumptions below are in play at all
--
-- The point of the file is that ONE GLOBAL SETTING IS THE WRONG SHAPE, and the first
-- two decisions demonstrate why by disagreeing about which reading is safe.
-- NEGATION-AS-FAILURE-SPEC.md assigns them by polarity: `presumed` (open world) for a
-- PERMISSION, `holds` (closed world) for discharging an OBLIGATION. Flip the radio
-- between these two and watch each one become dangerous in turn.
--
-- Nothing here evaluates: every atom is an uninterpreted ASSUME or a GIVEN parameter,
-- which is exactly what we want — a screenful of genuinely UNANSWERED atoms is the
-- input the grounding knob exists to interpret.

IMPORT prelude

-- Atoms nobody has answered.
--
-- Deliberately NOT under a `§` section: a name declared inside one is reported to the
-- visualizer fully qualified ("… (qualified at section X)"), which lands verbatim in the
-- box label and makes every leaf unreadably wide.

ASSUME `a prohibition notice is in force` IS A BOOLEAN
ASSUME `the visitor holds a valid pass`   IS A BOOLEAN
ASSUME `the site is a restricted area`    IS A BOOLEAN

ASSUME `the return was filed`             IS A BOOLEAN
ASSUME `the levy was paid`                IS A BOOLEAN
ASSUME `an extension was granted`         IS A BOOLEAN

ASSUME `the premises are residential`     IS A BOOLEAN
ASSUME `smoke alarms are fitted`          IS A BOOLEAN
ASSUME `the escape route is clear`        IS A BOOLEAN


-- == 1. A PERMISSION — whose two atoms want OPPOSITE readings ==

-- The sharpest argument against a single global switch, and it is visible inside ONE rule.
-- Neither reading is safe here, because the two atoms have opposite polarity:
--
--   * `a prohibition notice is in force` sits under a NOT, so reading it FALSE (closed
--     world) is the GENEROUS move — it declines to bar a visitor nobody has actually
--     barred. Open world invents a prohibition and locks them out.
--   * `the visitor holds a valid pass` is a positive precondition, so reading it FALSE is
--     the STRICT move, and open world is what invents the pass.
--
-- So closed world is right for one atom and wrong for the other, in the same conjunction.
-- That is why NEGATION-AS-FAILURE-SPEC.md puts `holds`/`presumed` at the USE SITE: the
-- drafter chooses per atom, and this knob can only ever supply a house default for the
-- atoms nobody chose for.
DECIDE `may enter the site` IF
        NOT `a prohibition notice is in force`
    AND     `the visitor holds a valid pass`


-- == 2. AN OBLIGATION — closed world is the safe reading ==

-- Exactly inverted. An unproven filing is NOT a filing: nobody discharges a duty by
-- failing to mention it. Open world here manufactures compliance out of silence, which
-- is the single most expensive mistake this picture can make.
DECIDE `has discharged the reporting duty` IF
            `the return was filed`
        AND `the levy was paid`
    OR      `an extension was granted`


-- == 3. A SEAM — scope ⇒ requirement, so the two lamps engage ==

-- Watch the LAMPS, not the wires (DESIGN §25.4). With grounding off, both lamps stay
-- dark and that is honest: undetermined. Closed world reads the unanswered scope as
-- out-of-scope and the note says N/A "on this reading" — never as a plain finding.
-- Open world puts the case in scope and then decides the requirement, which is how a
-- single knob can walk you from "we don't know" to "in breach" with no new facts.
DECIDE `rule 7 is satisfied` IF
    `the premises are residential`
        IMPLIES
            `smoke alarms are fitted`
        AND `the escape route is clear`


-- == 4. DEFAULTS — the other axis ==

-- `respect defaults` off demotes each TYPICALLY from a value to a mere mark: the box
-- stays fine-dashed and keeps its tag (so you can still see WHERE a presumption was
-- available) while the value it supplied disappears. Answer: how much of this verdict
-- was resting on presumptions rather than facts?
--
-- Turn defaults OFF and grounding to CLOSED and every leaf here reads
-- "assumed false (typically off)" — both things that happened to it, named.
GIVEN
  `the applicant is of age`     IS A BOOLEAN TYPICALLY TRUE
  `the applicant is bankrupt`   IS A BOOLEAN TYPICALLY FALSE
  `a guarantor has signed`      IS A BOOLEAN TYPICALLY FALSE
  `the deposit has cleared`     IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the tenancy may proceed` IF
        `the applicant is of age`
    AND NOT `the applicant is bankrupt`
    AND `the deposit has cleared`
    AND `a guarantor has signed`


-- == 5. Brute permutations ==

-- No story, just coverage: every combinator the kernel draws, over atoms that are all
-- unanswered, so each radio setting has something of every shape to act on.
-- (Mixed provenance in `perm4` is the interesting one: a presumption and an assumption
-- meeting on the same rung, which must stay visually distinguishable — amber for what
-- the SOURCE presumed, slate-blue for what the READER assumed.)

GIVEN p IS A BOOLEAN
      q IS A BOOLEAN
      r IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `perm1 — pure series` IF
        p
    AND q
    AND r

GIVEN p IS A BOOLEAN
      q IS A BOOLEAN
      r IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `perm2 — pure parallel` IF
        p
    OR  q
    OR  r

GIVEN p IS A BOOLEAN
      q IS A BOOLEAN
      r IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `perm3 — negation over a group` IF
    NOT     p
        AND q
    OR      r

GIVEN p IS A BOOLEAN TYPICALLY TRUE
      q IS A BOOLEAN
      r IS A BOOLEAN TYPICALLY FALSE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `perm4 — presumed and assumed on one rung` IF
        p
    AND q
    AND NOT r

GIVEN p IS A BOOLEAN
      q IS A BOOLEAN
      r IS A BOOLEAN
      s IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `perm5 — sum of products` IF
            p
        AND q
    OR      r
        AND s

GIVEN p IS A BOOLEAN
      q IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `perm6 — seam with a compound scope` IF
            p
        AND q
    IMPLIES
            NOT p
        OR  q
